Date: Wed, 10 Feb 93 12:32:29 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: POLI: Collectivist justification

Tom Morrow writes:
>I have little doubt that if the matter were put to a public referendum
>today most Americans would expressly consent to the jurisdiction of the US
>Constitution.  That you would not does not preclude *them* from doing so,
>and they quite rightfully object to claims that the US Constitution cannot
>be justified *at all*.  Libertarians who imply otherwise are actually using
>a collectivist argument: "Because I do not consent to statism, nobody else
>can either." ... You can choose to live under statism, and indeed
>many people do.  For reasons I will be happy to outline, I think that this
>it is unwise and unecessary to accept statism.  But I will respect your
>choice do so.  I ask only that you in turn respect my right to deny my
>allegiance to the institutions you and other statists agree to live under."

I'm with Tom.  We are selling a (largely vaporware!) product.  Today
most folks are buying competing, and we claim poorer, products.  Most
don't realize they are even making a choice, but if asked would indeed
choose the current products.  And by doing so indirectly impose great
costs on us to choose the product we would prefer.  To overcome this,
we have to sell people on the idea that our product would better give
them the things they want.  Any moralizing that doesn't come down to
this is beside the point, in my opinion.

Robin Hanson

