Date: Wed, 10 Feb 93 12:44:11 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: POLI: Buy Out Statists (Was: married to the State)

Tom Morrow writes:
>This analysis also tells us that we ought to try to make it less expensive
>for statists to give up their side of the bargain if we want to convince
>them to let us go peaceably.  Regardless of how you view the ethics of
>"buying off the enemey", it has been used with some success in places
>recently freed of Soviet control.  Here in the US a similar policy would
>allow statists to become owners and operators of the services they now hold
>as civil servants.  Air traffic controllers, for example, will be more
>likely to support less government if they are assured that they will be
>allowed to hold onto their radars and compete with newcomers.

If PPL is in fact a more economically efficient solution, there must
exist a win-win deal that benefits all parties.  Since statists are
going to be uncertain about whether in fact PPL is better, they should
get assets whose value doesn't depend strongly on PPLs being better.
PPL advocates should instead suffer this risk.

The problem is of course the negotiation costs - finding a deal most
everyone thinks is better than the present deal and is self-enforcing,
and convincing folks to stop jockying for an even better deal.  For
those who like to design deals, here is your ultimate challenge!

Robin Hanson

