> 
> I hope I am not crazy, just afraid. Your point is well taken, but
> I welcome your all'
> s thinking about this -- I claim that we have accepted the idea
> that you can force a good change in a society, when I say "we" I mean
> Americans, not extropians, and when I say force I mean brutal physical
> force, beating and killing people. 
> Krzys'

I have to take issue with you on two points.  On the more important one,
if you just said "we accept etc." I couldn't argue with you (of course
I'd want to emphatically repeat your proviso that "we" doesn't mean you
and me).
     But when you say "we have accepted" it implies a change, that there
was some previous time when we or our predecessors didn't countenance any
such idea.  I can't think when that time would have been, at least as
far back as the Holy Inquisition. 
     But my earlier objection was on other grounds.  I don't think it's
necessary, or helpful, to appeal to notions like that to explain police
brutality.  Police beating up undesirables is a widespread phenomenon in
all times and places, and I think has two roots that have little or
nothing to do with general social ideology.  One, unfortunately, is
just human--or at least male--nature:  males in groups have a bit of
a tendency toward violence, and the nature of police work is conducive
to that tendency.  And the other is repressive rather than reformative.
Surely the cops who beat up King weren't thinking that they were going
to reform or improve him by doing so?   Except in the sense of making
him sufficiently afraid of policeman that he'd never give one a hard
time again.

Scott DeLancey



