Subject: BOOK: A Fire upon the Deep

> At first I, too, thought that the book was not /particularly/
> anarcho-capitalist, but on brief reflection I now realize that that is because
> the anarcho-capitalism is an /assumption/ of the background, and is not
> talked about explicitly.  But it is there, everywhere.

Same tack I'm taking in my nearly completed SF novel, _Shadows_And_Stars_.
Libertarian SF tends to suffer from excessive tub-thumping.  Fortunately,
Vinge is smart enough to avoid this, and (he said modestly) so am I.

Now, whether I can *write fiction* well enough to sell a novel is another
question :-).  I've proven to my own satisfaction that I can sell as much
nonfiction as I care to write; I'm really good at that.

So far, though, my efforts to sell short fiction have come up a big fat zilch.
Which is frustrating, because I *know* I'm writing better stuff than some
of what gets published in the major SF markets.

Prospects for the novel look good, though.  It's got hard-SF-like worldbuilding
and an adventure plot, a combination I've been told the SF imprints really
like to see because there's a steady market for it and not many people who
can write it competently.  We'll see where it goes.
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

